<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bosnia and Herzegovina</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bosnia-Herzegovina, Bosnia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> is a country in Southeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> on the Balkan Peninsula, with a short coast on the Adriatic Sea. The Banate of Bosnia was established in the 12th century and became the Kingdom of Bosnia; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> annexed the region in 1463, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> ruled it from the late 19th century until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> it was a republic of Socialist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> and in 1992, following the breakup of Yugoslavia, proclaimed independence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnian-war">Bosnian War</a> that followed lasted until late 1995 and ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayton-agreement">Dayton Agreement</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina.jpg" alt="Bosnia and Herzegovina" width="330" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mladen Kolobarić · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bosnia_and_Herzegovina.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Badanj Cave near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stolac">Stolac</a> holds one of the oldest known cave engravings, an animal figure believed to be a horse, dated to about 13,000 to 12,000 BCE. The Neolithic Butmir culture, flourishing about 5100 to 4500 BCE, is known for its ceramics and anthropomorphic figurines. Most of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> was part of the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> by the mid-first century BCE. Slavs raided the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> in the 6th and early 7th centuries, and the exact identity of the tribes recorded as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> in the second quarter of the 7th century is debated by scholars. The name Bosona first appears in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-administrando-imperio">De Administrando Imperio</a>, written by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> between 948 and 952.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> incorporated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> as a province with its historical name and territorial integrity, introducing a new landholding system, reorganised administrative units and social differentiation by class and religion. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-berlin">Congress of Berlin</a> in 1878 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyula-andrassy">Gyula Andrássy</a> obtained Austro-Hungarian occupation and administration of Bosnia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> it became a province of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, soon renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the whole region was ceded to the Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, a German and Italian puppet state led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaše</a>, whose leaders ran a campaign of extermination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a> and set up death camps. An estimated 209,000 Serbs, 16.9 per cent of the Serb population, were killed in the territory. Within socialist Yugoslavia Bosnia hosted much of the military defence industry, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a> held the Winter Olympics from 7 to 19 February 1984. Multi-party elections were held on 18 and 25 November 1990, and a split opened between Serbs, who largely favoured staying in Yugoslavia, and Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>, who favoured independence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> has a coastline of about 20 kilometres around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neum">Neum</a>. Sarajevo is the capital and largest city. The 2013 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 3,531,159 people, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosniaks">Bosniaks</a> 50.1 per cent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> 30.8 per cent and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> 15.5 per cent; Republika Srpska officials contest the figures. The state comprises the Federation of Bosnia and Herzegovina, Republika Srpska and the Brčko District, under a presidency with one member from each of the three main groups. Bosnian, Serbian and Croatian have equal status. Twenty stećak necropolis sites were added to the World Heritage List in 2006.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bosnia and Herzegovina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Efforts in 1991 to bring the Socialist Republic of <b>Bosnia and Herzegovina</b> into the federation, through talks between Milošević, Bosnia's Serbian Democratic Party and the Bosniak vice-president Adil Zulfikarpašić, ended by late 1991, when Izetbegović planned a referendum on independence and violence broke out between Serbs and Bosniaks.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 2016 about 700 troops were stationed abroad: 100 with the NATO-led ISAF force in Afghanistan, 210 in Kosovo under KFOR and 160 in <b>Bosnia and Herzegovina</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hungary">Hungary · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The NDH covered most of present-day Croatia, all of <b>Bosnia and Herzegovina</b>, part of Serbia and a small part of Slovenia, while Italy and Hungary took Dalmatia, the islands, Međimurje and southern Baranja.</blockquote><a href="/empire/independent-state-of-croatia">Independent State of Croatia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its annexation of <b>Bosnia and Herzegovina</b> in October 1908, where Serbia had hoped to expand, set off the Bosnian Crisis of 1908–1909, which permanently damaged relations between Austria-Hungary and Russia and Serbia and was among the causes of World War I. The Treaty of London of May 1913…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The six republics, <b>Bosnia and Herzegovina</b>, Croatia, Macedonia, Montenegro, Serbia and Slovenia, were constitutionally equal; Serbia alone contained two autonomous provinces, Kosovo and Vojvodina, which the 1974 constitution gave voting power equal to the republics', to the lasting anger of Serbian officials.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Federal constitutions have since been used to end or prevent conflict, as in <b>Bosnia and Herzegovina</b> in 1995, Iraq in 2005 and Nepal in 2015.</blockquote><a href="/government/federation">Federation · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
